## 1. Introduction
As a kind of highly hazardous contamination, arsenic (As) in the environment waters has potential toxic effects on the living organisms, and maybe cause cancerous health effects, such as bladder, lungs and skin cancers [1,2]. Recently, As is ubiquitous in the contaminated nature waters as a consequence of anthropogenic activities such as mining, smelting and wastewater discharge [3]. Generally, As mainly exists as dissolved inorganic forms of ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ and $\mathsf{A s}(\mathrm{III})$ . The inorganic As species are both toxic, but the As(III) is about 60 times more poisonous than ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ [4]. A maximum contaminate level of $10\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ for As in drinking water has been set by World Health Organization (WHO) [5,6]. Therefore, the effective As remediation from potential drinking water is significant to protect human health and ecological environment.
Various As remediation techniques, such as coprecipitation, adsorption, ion exchange, reverse osmosis and membrane filtration [7,8]. Adsorption is a simple, economical, safe, fast and environment-friendly technique for As remediation, particularly when the As concentrations are extremely low [9,10]. Martinson et al. [11] suggested that $\mathtt{C u O}$ had good adsorption capacities of $22.6\:\mathrm{mg/g}\:(\mathrm{As(V))}$ and $26.9\:\mathrm{mg/g}$ (As(III)). Cui et al. [12] reported amorphous zirconium oxide nanoparticles $\scriptstyle\left({\mathsf{a m}}-{\mathsf{Z r O}}_{2}\right)$ ) with the removal capacities of $32.4\mathrm{mg/g(As(V))}$ and $83\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}$ (As(III)). Andjelkovic et al. [13] showed graphene- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\upalpha}$ -FeOOH aerogel with adsorption capacities of $81.3\mathrm{mg/g}$ $(\mathsf{A}\mathsf{s}(\mathsf{V}))$ and $13.42\mathrm{mg/g}$ (As(III)). Benjwal et al. [14] synthesized the reduced graphene oxide-metal oxide based nanocomposites $(\mathrm{r}{\mathsf{G O-F e}_{3}}{\mathsf{O}_{4}}{\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}}$ ) with $\mathsf{A s}(\mathrm{III})$ adsorption capacity of $147.05\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Chen et al. [15] reported ${\mathfrak{\beta}}\qquad$ - FeOOH@GO-COOH nanocomposite with good adsorption capacities of $45.7\mathrm{mg/g(As(V))}$ and $77.5\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}$ (As(III)). These adsorbents are a limited performance for As remediation. Thus, it’s still urgent to develop promising adsorbents with relatively high As uptake capacity and chemical stability.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with structural diversity, crystalline nature, and pore tunability have received research interest because of the broad range of widespread applications, including chemical sensing [16–18], separation [19], gas adsorption [20], linear optical properties [21,22]. Recently, some stable microporous MOF materials have been greatly promoted the potential application and some of them are developed as adsorbents for separation of various hazardous contamination such as aromatic compounds [23], organic dyes [24], heavy metal ions [10,25] and alcohols [26] from water. Zr-based MOFs (UiO-66) were first synthesized by Cavka et al. and caused widespread research interest because of the excellent chemical, thermal and mechanical stability [27]. Yee et al. [28] have synthesized Zr-based MOFs for efficient removal of mercury from vapor phase and highly strong acid. He et al. [29] presented the excellent performance of Zr-based MOFs in removing antimony (Sb) ions. Sb and As are Group V elements, and share some similar physicochemical properties. Therefore, Zrbased MOFs are hopeful to promote the adsorption capacity for As.
Herein, zirconium metal-organic frameworks (UiO-66 and UiO-66 $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})\mathrm{;}$ have been successfully synthesized by a facile solvothermal technique and utilized to adsorb As from contaminated water. The removal process and performance of zirconium metal-organic frameworks have been investigated. The UiO-66 was found to have an exceptional As(III, V) removal performance in the fixed-bed column experiments. Additionally, XPS, FTIR and EXAFS analysis were applied to elucidate the mechanism of the As adsorption on Zr-based MOFs.
