## 2.4. Adsorption experiments
The batch experiments were performed at $\mathsf{p H}=9.2\pm0.1$ in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ stoppered conical flasks containing 0.01 M $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{B}_{4}\mathsf{O}_{7}$ solution. Adsorption isotherms of As(III, V) were taken out at 25, 35 and $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , respectively. Adsorbent $(20\mathrm{mg})$ was added into As solution $(20~\mathrm{mL}$ , then the mixtures were shaken at $150\mathrm{rpm}$ in an incubator shaker (IS-RDD3, Crystal Technology $\&$ Industries, Inc., USA) to achieve adsorption equilibrium for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The adsorbent was separated from the solution by centrifuging at $12,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $8\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, As concentration in supernatant was measured with an AFS, and the amount of As bound to the adsorbent was calculated by the formula as follow:
$$
\mathsf{Q}_{e}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})\times V}{W}
$$
where $Q_{e}(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ is adsorption capacity of As on adsorbent, $C_{0}\left(\mathrm{mg/}\right.$ $\mathbf{g}^{\star}$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}\left(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ are the initial and equilibrium As concentration in the supernatant, respectively, $V\left(\mathrm{mL}\right)$ is the volume of As suspension, W (g) is the adsorbent mass.
Adsorption kinetic was taken out to investigate the reaction time to reach adsorption equilibrium. Stirred at $400\mathrm{rpm}$ under $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $500\mathrm{mg}$ of adsorbent was dispersed in $500~\mathrm{mL}$ As solution. Then, samples were taken out at desired reaction time intervals, and the residual As was analyzed by an AFS.
## 2.5. Effect of pH and co-existing anions
To investigate the influence of pH on the As removal, the pH values of As solution were adjusted by adding proper amount of $0.1\mathrm{mol/LNaOH}$ or HCl at room temperature. Then, $50\mathrm{mg}$ of adsorbent was dispersed in $50\mathrm{mL}$ of As solution which had a desired pH value. The prepared mixtures were sealed and stirred at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
The effects of co-existing anions, including $C0_{3}^{2-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{B r}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , Sb(V), Sb(III), $\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{-}$ , on As(III, V) removal were evaluated by adding $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ adsorbent into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ As solution which contained co-existing anions. Then, all the prepared mixture was sealed shaken at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , the residual As was analyzed by an AFS.
## 2.6. Fixed-bed column experiments
Fixed-bed column experiments were applied by packing UiO-66 in $60\mathrm{mm}$ long PVC columns with a diameter of $15\mathrm{mm}$ . A total of $330\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of wet UiO-66 was packed in the PVC column. Flow rate was maintained by a constant flow pump. The superficial liquid velocities (SLV) for ${\sf A s}({\sf I I I})$ and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ were $62~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}/{\mathrm{min}}$ and $69\upmu\mathrm{L}/$ min, respectively. The As contaminated water with a concentration of 100 ppb was used.
## 2.7. XAS analyses
XAS was applied to determine the valence state of ${\mathsf{A s}}({\mathrm{III}},\ \mathsf{V})$ adsorbed on UiO-66. As(III, V) K-edge XAS spectra performed at the Beamline 4W1B in the BSRF (Beijing Synchrotron Radiation Facility). The near-edge spectra with 11,666–12,868 eV were scanned to obtain the As(III, V) K-edge XAS spectrum. The obtained data were analyzed using ARTEMIS and ATHENA software, version 1.2.11 [30].
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Adsorbent characterization
The morphology and microstructure of the adsorbents were characterized by the TEM and SEM. UiO-66 and UiO- $66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ exhibits highly separated and homogeneously small size (Fig. 1). Fig. 1a reveals the smooth surface of UiO-66, while Fig. 1b presents the rough surfaces of UiO- $66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ , this phenomenon may be attributed to the presence of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ group on UiO-66 $\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ [29]. The UiO-66 and $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ are well dispersed and exhibited the uniformed size (Fig. 1c and d), and the average diameter was approximately $50\mathrm{nm}$ .
The BET surface areas were determined based on nitrogen adsorption-desorption method. The BET surface area (Fig. S2a) of the UiO-66 is $485.9\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ , while BET surface area of the UiO-66 $\left(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ decreases remarkably to $113.4\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The introduction of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ group in the space of the micropores lead to the BET surface area decrease of UiO-66 $\begin{array}{r}{\left\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right\rangle}\end{array}$ [31]. The total pore volume is 0.493 and $0.332\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ for UiO-66 and $\mathsf{U i O-}66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ , respectively (Fig. S3a and b). The higher cumulative pore volume of UiO-66 was mainly caused by the mesoporosity on the surface, resulting in larger amount of adsorption sites on UiO-66. The obtained UiO-66 and UiO- $66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ were characterized by XRD. The XRD patterns of obtained Zr-based MOFs (Fig. 2) are in good accordance with the UiO-66 simulated pattern [32,33]. The XRD patterns of As containing Zr-based MOFs are presented in Fig. S1. After the
As adsorption, the position of the main peaks for Zr-based MOFs retains no shift, demonstrating no change of the phase for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs.
FTIR spectra of obtained $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs samples are presented in Fig. S2b. Compared with UiO-66 samples, peaks at 3366, 3480, 1340, $1624\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of UiO-66 $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2})$ are assigned to amine symmetrical stretching vibration, amine asymmetrical stretching vibration, $\mathsf{C-N}$ stretching and $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ bending vibration [34], respectively, revealing the successful synthesis of UiO-66 $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right\rangle$ . The FTIR spectra of As containing Zr-based MOFs were investigated to reveal the adsorption mechanism. As shown in Fig. 3, the characteristic adsorption peaks at 486 and $484~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ vibration on Zr-based MOFs [35]. After As(III, V) adsorption, the $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ vibration shifted to the lower wavenumbers, while no significant changes were observed at the peak at 1571 and $1624\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to benzene skeleton and $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ bending vibration, respectively. These results demonstrate that $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ plays a vital role in As adsorption in this study. As shown in Fig. S3c and d, UiO-66 and UiO- $.66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ had total weight loss of $49.58\%$ and $59.10\%$ , respectively, under the experimental condition, which was close to the previous report [27,36]. The ‘‘node” of UiO-66 and UiO-66 $\left(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ mainly formed zirconium oxides in at the end of the decomposition.
The activation process of UiO-66 has been depicted in Test S4. As shown in Fig. S4(a), the activated UiO-66 shows much higher BET surface area than the as-synthesized UiO-66 [27]. The BET surface area of the activated UiO-66 is $1165\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , which is close to the
Langmuir surface area of UiO-66. Compared with the TGA curve of as-synthesized UiO-66, Fig. S4(b) presents the first weight loss of activated UiO-66 between 25 and $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a lower weight loss of $8.90\%$ , which is attributed to the removal of the residual DMF/ un-coordinated ligand molecules during the activation process. The activated UiO-66 had a fast weight loss between 450 and $550^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , which was attributed to the decomposition of p-Phthalic acid. The activated UiO-66 had the total weight loss of $61.75\%$ under the experimental condition. As shown in Fig. S4(c), the disappearance of the peak at $1660\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is ascribed to the removal of DMF during the activation process. The peaks at around $3000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is due to benzene ring’s aromatic and aliphatic $\upsilon$ (CAH) modes. The peaks at around $3675\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the isolated OH groups of the external surfaces, while the peak at around $3440\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is ascribed to the inter-crystalline water [27]. Fig. S4(d) reveals that the XRD pattern of activated UiO-66 is in good accordance with the simulated pattern.
## 3.2. Adsorption isotherms
The UiO-66 and UiO-66( $\left[\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right]$ exhibit excellent adsorption performance in the low initial As concentration (Fig. S5), the ${\sf A s}({\sf I I I})$ and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ removal efficiencies of UiO-66 $(91.83\%$ and $89.80\%$ ， respectively) is higher than that of UiO- $.66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ $(73.47\%$ and $75.51\%$ , respectively), revealing the adsorption performance of UiO-66 is superior to UiO-66 $\left[\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right]$ in the low initial As concentration. The adsorption isotherms of As on UiO-66 at 25, 35 and $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ are presented in Fig. 4. The adsorption capacity of As on UiO-66 increased gradually as the initial As concentrations increase. The increase in adsorption temperature from 25 to $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ results in As (III) and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ maximum adsorption capacities falling from 205.0 to $165.65\mathrm{mg/g}$ and 68.21 to $41.81\mathrm{mg/g},$ respectively. Similar tendency is observed in the As adsorption on UiO- $.66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ (Fig. 5). It revealed that As adsorption capacities of zirconium metalorganic frameworks decreased with increasing temperature under the experimental conditions.
The equilibrium data were simulated by Langmuir (Eq. (2)), Freundlich (Eq. (3)) and Dubinin-Radushkevich (D-R) (Eq. (4)) isotherm models to determine the essence of As removal process. The mathematical representations of Langmuir, and Freundlich isotherm models are given in Eq. (2) and (3), respectively:
$$
\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{e}}=\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{\mathrm{max}}}+\frac{1}{k_{L}Q_{\mathrm{max}}}
$$
$$
Q_{e}=k_{f}C_{e}^{\frac{1}{n}}
$$
where $Q_{\mathrm{max}}$ is the maximum As adsorption capacity $\left({\mathrm{mg/g}}\right)$ , $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the equilibrium As concentration $\mathrm{(mg/L)}$ , $Q_{\mathrm{e}}$ represents the amount of As $\scriptstyle({\mathrm{mg}}/{\mathrm{g}})$ adsorbed at equilibrium, $k_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $k_{\mathrm{f}}$ are the Langmuir constant $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mg})$ and Freundlich constant $(\mathrm{m}\mathbf{g}^{1-(1/n)}~\mathbf{L}^{1/n}~\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ , respectively; $n$ represents the Freundlich parameter.
$Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based MOFs is monolayer coverage. The UiO-66’s theoretical As adsorption capacities obtained from the Langmuir model at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ are 204.38 and $68.19\mathrm{mg/g}$ for $\mathsf{A s}(\mathrm{III})$ and $\mathsf{A s}(\mathsf{V})$ , respectively, which are well identical with the experimental value of $205.0\mathrm{mg/g}$ for As (III) and $68.2\mathrm{mg/g}$ for ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ . As shown in Table 2, UiO-66 exhibits the excellent As uptake performance, the As(III) removal capacity of UiO-66 $(205.0\mathrm{mg/g)}$ was much higher than that of magnetite nanoparticles $(18.70\mathrm{mg/g})$ [3], am- $.Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ $(83\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ [12], graphene- $\mathfrak{a}$ -FeOOH aerogel $(13.42\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ [13], rGO $\scriptstyle\mathtt{\mathtt{)-F e_{3}O_{4}-T i O_{2}}}$ $(147.05\mathrm{mg/g)}$ [14], $\upbeta$ -FeOOH@GO-COOH nanocomposite $(77.5\:\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ [15], $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}\mathrm{-RGO-MnO_{2}}}$ nanocomposites $(14.04\mathrm{mg/g)}$ [34], ${\mathrm{RGO-ZrO}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}$ ${\bf\langle95.15\ m g/g\rangle}$ [35], rice polish $(0.139\mathrm{mg/g)}$ [42], activate carbon nanotubes $(9.74\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ [43], $\mathfrak{a}$ -FeOOH@GCA $(56.43\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ [44], MIO-CNTs $(47.41\ \mathrm{mg/g)}$ [45] and functionalized graphene sheets $(138.79\mathrm{mg/g)}$ [46]. Moreover, UiO-66’s As removal capacities are much higher than that of pure zirconia of 3.65 and $2.56\:\mathrm{mg/g}$ for ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ and ${\sf A s}({\sf I I I})$ [47], respectively, which demonstrated that UiO-66 had significantly stronger affinity with As(III) and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ .
The equilibrium data were analyzed by D-R isotherm model to determine the mechanism of ${\sf A s}({\sf I I I})$ and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ adsorption (chemisorption or physisorption). The D-R isotherm model’s linear equation [48] (Eq. (4)) is listed as follows:
$$
\mathsf{l n}q_{e}=\mathsf{l n}q_{m}-\beta\varepsilon^{2}
$$
where $q_{\mathrm{e}}\ ({\mathrm{mol/g}})$ is the amount of As sorbed per unit weight of adsorbent; $q_{\mathrm{m}}({\bf m o l}/{\bf g})$ is the maximum removal capacity; $\beta$ $(\mathrm{mol}^{2}/\mathrm{J}^{2})$ is the activity coefficient and e $\left(\varepsilon=R T\mathrm{ln}(1+1/C_{e})\right)$ is the Polanyi potential.
Table 3, Fig. S8 and S9 show that the equilibrium data are in good agreement with the D-R isotherm model. The mean free energy of adsorption is expressed by Eq. (5):
$$
E=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\beta}}
$$
The value of $E\left(\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right.$ ) determines the nature of As adsorption mechanism. Table 3 shows the free energy values (9.71, 13.40, 9.77 and $11.92\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ) are between 8 and $16\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ [49], demonstrating that As species adsorption onto zirconium metal-organic frameworks are chemical in nature.
## 3.3. Thermodynamic calculations
Thermodynamic parameters including: Gibbs free energy $\Delta G^{0}$ $(\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}\cdot$ ), enthalpy $\Delta H^{0}(\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}};$ , and entropy $\Delta S^{0}$ (kJ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ) [50–52], can be calculated as follows:
$$
\Delta G^{0}=-R T\mathrm{ln}K
$$
$$
\mathrm{ln}K=-\frac{\Delta H^{0}}{R T}+\frac{\Delta S^{0}}{R}
$$
where $T$ is temperature in Kelvin (K), R represents the universal gas constant $(8.314\mathrm{J}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1};$ , $K$ signifies the dimensionless equilibrium coefficient, and $K$ is estimated using the Langmuir parameter $(K_{\mathrm{L}})$ [53] as
$$
K=K_{L}\times C_{w}
$$
where $C_{w}$ $(5.56\times10^{4}\quad\mathrm{mmol}\quad\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ represents the water concentration.
## 3.4. Adsorption kinetics
The removal rate is a vital factor for As-contaminated water treatment and optimizing operation conditions in practical applications. The process of As uptake by UiO-66 as a function of time was investigated. The results are shown in Fig. 6, investigations reveal that UiO-66 rapidly absorbs As. The adsorption kinetics of As by UiO-66 were fast within the initial $10\mathrm{min}$ , then the As adsorption capacities gradually achieved equilibrium within $20\mathrm{min}$ . The As removal rate is significantly faster than many other adsorbents, such as synthetic siderite [6], $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles [10] and rice polish [43]. In the low initial As concentration, Fig. S12 shows that the adsorption processes of As(III) and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ are very fast within the initial $10\mathrm{min}$ , then gradually achieves equilibrium within $20\mathrm{min}$ . The high adsorption rate is attributed to the presence of adequate adsorption sites on UiO-66 and UiO- $.66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ .
To further investigate the mechanism of As adsorption kinetic, the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models [54] were applied to analyze the adsorption kinetic data. The pseudo-first-order, and pseudo-second-order are expressed as Eq. (6) and (7), respectively.
$$
\ln(Q_{e}-Q_{t})=\ln Q_{e}-K_{1}t
$$
$$
\frac{t}{{\cal Q}_{t}}=\frac{t}{{\cal Q}_{e}}+\frac{1}{K_{2}{\cal Q}_{e}^{2}},\quad\mathrm{where}h_{0}=K_{2}\cdot{\cal Q}_{e}^{2}
$$
where $Q_{\mathrm{t}}$ and $Q_{\mathrm{e}}$ represent the amount $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ of As adsorbed on UiO-66 at time t (min) and at equilibrium, respectively; $K_{1}$ and $K_{2}$ are the adsorption rate constants; $h_{0}$ represents the initial adsorption rate $(\mathrm{mg~mg}^{-1}\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$ ).
## 3.5. Effect of environmental media
## 3.5.1. Effect of pH
The effect of solution pH (1.0–13.5) on the As adsorption capacities of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs is presented in Fig. 7. Similar tendency of As (III, V) adsorption performance of Zr-based MOFs is clearly observed. For As(III), the adsorption capacity maintains relatively low at $\mathsf{p H}$ values from 1.0 to 7.0, then, increases quickly at the values of pH from 7.0 to 9.0, the adsorbents had the largest adsorption capacity at ${\sf p H}=9.0$ , finally, a tremendous drop in the ${\sf A s}({\sf I I I})$ adsorption is found at the values of pH from 9.0 to 13.5 (Fig. 7a). For ${\mathsf{A s}}(\mathsf{V}).$ at the $\mathsf{p H}$ values from 1.0 to 11.0, the adsorption capacity maintains relatively high, a tremendous decrease in the ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ removal is found at $\mathsf{p H}$ from 11.0 to 13.5 (Fig. 7b). Zeta-potential measurements (Fig. S13) show the $\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{pzc}}$ values of Zr-based MOFs are 7.2 and 8.5, respectively. For As(III), $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{A s O}_{3}$ is the most common species when $\mathsf{p H}$ are below 9.2, at higher $\mathsf{p H}$ values, the $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{AsO}_{3}$ species gradually dissociate into ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{AsO}}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{HAsO}_{3}^{2-}$ , and ${\sf A s O}_{3}^{3-}$ with the increasing pH. For $\mathsf{A s}(\mathsf{V})$ , $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{AsO}_{4}$ is the most common species for the pH below 6.9, the $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{AsO}_{4}$ species change from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{AsO}_{4}^{-}$ to $\mathrm{HAsO}_{4}^{2-}$ with the increasing $\mathsf{p H}$ , and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ occurs as ${\mathsf{A s O}}_{4}^{3-}$ when $\mathsf{p H}$ is above 8. At the acidic solution, the surface charge of the zirconium metal-organic frameworks is positive due to the protonation reaction, For As(III), there is little electrostatic attraction between the uncharged arsenite species $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{A s O}_{3}$ and adsorbent, and the As(III) adsorption capacity is relatively low at pH values from 1.0 to 7.0. For ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{A s O}_{4}^{-}$ is dominant at pH values less than about 6.9, there is strong electrostatic adsorption between the negative charged arsenate species $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{AsO}_{4}^{-}$ and adsorbent, and the ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ adsorption capacity is relatively high at low pH. At strong alkalinity, because of the strong electrostatic repulsion between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{A}s\mathrm{O}_{3}^{-}/\mathrm{HA}s\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ and zirconium metal-organic frameworks, the As(III, V) adsorption capacities are relatively low in the strong alkalinity conditions.
## 3.5.2. Effects of coexisting anions
Coexisting anions, including $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ , ${\mathsf N}{\mathsf O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{B r}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , Sb(V), Sb (III), $\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{2-}$ and ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf P}{\sf O}_{4}^{-}$ , are ubiquitous in the environment. Although the coexisting anions are known as environmentfriendly, the anions maybe compete with As for specific adsorption sites on adsorbents. Therefore, the influence on As adsorption capacities in presence of coexisting anions is necessary to study. Fig. 8 shows that the existence of most coexisting anions $(\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{B r}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathsf{S b}(\mathsf{V})$ , Sb(III) and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ ) has no remarkable influence on the As removal. The results reveal that these coexisting anions don’t remarkably compete with As for the adsorption sites on
$Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs. A remarkable drop in the As removal capacities is observed in presence of phosphate $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}P O_{4}^{-}}$ and $\mathsf{H P O}_{4}^{2-}$ ). The phosphate inhibits the removal of As by competing the specific adsorption sites. This phenomenon can be attributed to the similar physicochemical properties of As and phosphate (both Group V elements). The results reveal that the As removal by $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs is prominently specific adsorption in presence of most coexisting anions except phosphate.
## 3.6. Regeneration
To investigate the stability and reusability of the $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -base MOFs, the regeneration processes were repeated six times to evaluate the potential practical applications of Zr-base MOFs (Fig. S14). $100\mathrm{mg}$ of As containing $Z\mathrm{r}$ -base MOFs was treated with $200\mathrm{mL}$ of $50\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA $\mathrm{(pH}=3\mathrm{)}$ , followed by stirring at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for three times. Compared to the initial cycle, the adsorption capacity of UiO-66 only decreased by $9.52\%$ and $13.61\%$ for As(III) and ${\mathsf{A s}}(\mathsf{V}).$ respectively, the adsorption capacity of UiO-66( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\cdot$ ) decreased by $13.15\%$ and $17.39\%$ for As(III) and ${\mathsf{A s}}(\mathsf{V}).$ respectively, revealing the good regeneration capacity of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -base MOFs.
## 3.7. XPS study
The chemical composition of Zr-based MOFs after and before As adsorption was analyzed by the XPS technique. As presented in Figs. 9 and S15, peaks corresponding to O 1s, C 1s, As 3d, and Zr 3d are obviously identified in the spectrum, indicating that the presence of the elements (C, O, As, and $Z\mathrm{r}$ ) on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs. Peak with binding energy of $285.01\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to C 1s [55], it has no obvious change after As adsorption. The specific peaks with binding energy of 45.27 and $46.36\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to As $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and As $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , indicating that $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs adsorbed ${\sf A s}({\sf I I I})$ and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ [55,56]. For the UiO-66, the proportion of $\mathrm{\Delta0_{ad}/O_{l a t t}}$ is $13.1\%$ before the As adsorption while the proportion of $\ensuremath{0_{\mathrm{ad}}}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{latt}}}$ has increased to $19.13\%$ and $21.83\%$ after the $\mathsf{A s}(\mathrm{III})$ and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ adsorption, respectively. For UiO-66 $\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , the proportion of $\mathrm{\Delta0_{ad}/O_{l a t t}}$ is $14.85\%$ before the As adsorption while the proportion of $\ensuremath{0_{\mathrm{ad}}}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{O_{\mathrm{latt}}}}$ has increased to $20.65\%$ and $18.80\%$ after the As(III) and $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{s}(\mathsf{V})$ adsorption, respectively. The phenomenon is prominently attributed to the fact that As is associated with $-\mathsf{O H}$ , then, lead to the increase of the $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{ad}/O_{l a t t}}$ proportion after As adsorption. As presented in Fig. S15e, peaks with binding energy of $400.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $399.6\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to N 1s, however, these peaks have no significant change after As adsorption. As presented in Figs. 9d and S15d, the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr~}}3\ensuremath{\mathrm{d}}$ peak can be decomposed into four peaks [57]. The specific peaks at $182.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $185.3\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bonds, the peaks at $185.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $183.5\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the $Z\mathrm{r}{-}Z\mathrm{r}$ bonds. It is obvious that these peaks correspond to $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bonds shift to the lower values after As adsorption. This results demonstrate that $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bonds has played a vital role in As removal, which are in good agreement with the FTIR spectra analysis.
## 3.8. Adsorption mechanisms studied by EXAFS
XANES spectroscopy was applied to determine the As oxidation state anchored on UiO-66. The spectra of As K-edge XANES shows that the adsorption edge of the As(III)-UiO-66 and As(V)-UiO-66 are at the same adsorption edge, which were in good agreement with the reference As(III) and As(V) spectra with adsorption maximum at 11871.8 and $11875.0\mathrm{eV}$ respectively. Therefore, the As (III) and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ were anchored on UiO-66 without the change of oxidation state. Fig. 10 shows the RDF profile for As-adsorbed UiO-66 samples, the fitted constants including the Debye-Waller factor $(\upsigma^{2})$ , interatomic distance (R), and coordination number (CN) are shown in Table S4. The first As-O coordination shell was simulated with ${\sim}3.2$ and ${\sim}4.4$ oxygen atoms of 1.77 and $1.69\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in As(III)-UiO66 and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ -UiO-66, respectively. Furthermore, the AsAO shell was contribute from 2.4 and 4 oxygen atoms at 1.80 and $1.70\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in trivalent and pentavalent arsenic, respectively. In the Zr-As coordination shells of As(III)-UiO-66, the As atom is surrounded by Zr atoms at $3.22\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ with a CN of 1.61 for As(III)-UiO-66, revealing the main formation of bidentate binuclear complexes (Fig. 11(a)). Similar observation was identified by Raven et al., As(III) usually forms bidentate complexes [58,59]. However, ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ mainly forms monodentate complexes [60]. The ${\sf A s}{-}Z{\sf r}$ shell of ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ -UiO-66 were contributed from $9.10~\mathrm{Zr}$ atoms at $2.95\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , which did not match the monodentate mononuclear complexes $({\sim}3.5\mathring\mathrm{A})$ of As (V) adsorption on zirconyl [61]. Thus, the As-Zr shell at $2.95\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ was more likely to contributed to the main formation of bidentate mononuclear complexes (Fig. 11(b)).
## 3.9. Fixed-bed column experiments
The fixed-bed column adsorption is more efficient than the batch process to confirm utilization of adsorbability. To evaluate the real applicability of UiO-66 for the As(III, V) removal in water samples, the fixed-bed column adsorption experiments were conducted by treating the simulated dilute industrial wastewater of initial As(III, V) concentration of $100{\mathrm{ppb}}$ . The composition of the simulated dilute industrial wastewater is summarized in Table S5. As shown in Fig. 12, a similar tendency is found for the breakthrough curves of As(V) and As(III), at the beginning, As(III, V) concentration in the effluent through the fixed-bed column cannot be obviously determined due to the detection limit of AFS, the As(III) and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ effective treatment volume for UiO-66 are as high as 2270 and 1775 BVs, respectively, until the maximum permissible concentration of 10 ppb was reached. This observation may be attributed to the large BET surface area and highly porous structure of UiO-66 which enhances contact efficiency of UiO-66 with As in aqueous solution. Even the initial As(III) concentration is as high as 20000 ppb, the As(III) effective treatment volume for UiO-66 are approximately 60 BVs. Thus, the immobilization of UiO-66 in fixed bed has good removal performance for As from the contaminated water. This approach can enhance the removal of As from water, and promote the application in effluent reactor, which is vital to its potential application in real practice.
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, zirconium metal-organic frameworks (UiO-66 and $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2});$ have been developed for the removal of As from contaminated water, the adsorption kinetics and adsorption isotherm of As on UiO-66 and UiO-66 $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2})$ were well fitted by the pseudosecond-order kinetic model, and Langmuir isotherm model, respectively. UiO-66 had the theoretical maximum As(III) and As (V) adsorption capacities of 205.0 and $68.21\ \mathrm{mg/g}$ , respectively, at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the ${\sf A s}({\sf I I I})$ uptake capacity of UiO-66 was much higher than many other adsorbents [34,35,62–67]. UiO-66 also exhibited excellent adsorption performance in the low initial As concentration and presented the excellent ability to remove As(III, V) in the fixed-bed column experiments. The adsorption performance of zirconium metal-organic frameworks was excellent with a relatively wide pH range for As(III) (7.0–9.0) and As(V) (1.0–11.0). The adsorbents also presented the excellent anti-interference ability that co-existing anions (except phosphate) have no obvious effect on the adsorption capacity. The adsorption performance of UiO66 and $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ remained stable after six adsorptiondesorption tests. The similar adsorption tendency of UiO-66 and $\mathsf{U i O-}66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ could reveal the similar adsorption mechanism. The As adsorption processes were identified to be chemisorption and exothermic reaction according to the calculation of Gibbs free energy and D-R isotherm model. XPS and FTIR study demonstrated that the bonds of $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ played a significant role in As removal. EXAFS spectra revealed the main formation of bidentate mononuclear complexes for ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ and bidentate binuclear complexes for As(III) on the hexanuclear Zr cluster of UiO-66. The results demonstrate that the highly stable and nontoxic zirconium metal-organic frameworks can be a promising candidate for As sequestration. With the excellent adsorption and separation ability, zirconium metal-organic frameworks can be developed for remediation of other pollutants in the future.
